WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:03.250
- While I was traveling in New England

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:06.143
I found myself surrounded by people.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:11.530
I hadn't been surrounded by
people for a very long time

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.960
and it felt weird, very weird, not least

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.900
because there's a pandemic
and there's a virus.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.160
And it just was like, is this okay?

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.010
Luckily we were following all the state

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.180
and local guidelines, being smart,

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:24.220
but I found myself going through

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.760
this really weird mind process.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.610
First being scared that
I was around people

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.410
and then being like, oh,
but I'm wearing a mask

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:32.750
and so it's probably fine.

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:34.650
I'm wearing a mask, mask to protect me,

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.740
but then being like, but
do masks actually work?

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.940
Didn't the CDC and WHO
come out and say like,

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.800
don't buy masks, masks aren't
the most important thing here.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:44.660
Which was really annoying that they

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.590
then reversed their position.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.040
Anyway it got me just thinking,

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.780
what does a mask actually do?

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.450
And is it actually protecting me

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.030
when I am around other people?

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:55.380
And I did what I always
do when this sort of thing

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.960
comes up and I went on a deep dive.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.820
- Put surgical masks on.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.860
- And I want to explain to
you four major takeaways

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.573
I've had about masks.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:09.120
By the way, this really cool
green mask that I'm wearing

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.520
and a bunch of other ones
that you'll see me wearing

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.540
in this video come from
my friends over at BioSkin

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.410
who make really nice, comfortable masks

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.090
that are breathable and wonderful

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:20.620
and I'm gonna talk about them later.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.710
For now let's talk about
the science of masks.

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:25.410
Takeaway number one:

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.700
your breath is kind of
like a rocking chair.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:29.550
- [Man] Ignition.

00:01:31.389 --> 00:01:32.420
- Bear with me on this one.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.310
I promise it will make sense.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:36.440
Watch this guy sneeze.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:40.900
It's kind of gross, but this
process is like a rocket ship

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:45.900
for a virus who wants to
escape the orbit of your body,

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:49.080
make it out into space
and land in the orbit

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:52.300
of somebody else's body
through this rocket ship,

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.510
these respiratory droplets,
sometimes that stuff lands

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:59.380
on surfaces like a doorknob
and you touch that surface

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.410
and that's how it makes
it into your world.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:05.253
Hence the mantra we've
heard a million times.

00:02:06.516 --> 00:02:08.960
But as we got into this virus,
more and more scientists

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.480
started to study how the
virus actually moves around

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.940
and they started to say
with a loud megaphone

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.520
as loudly as they could,

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.400
the virus doesn't just
spread through droplets.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.110
It also spreads through aerosols.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.840
And all of us are like,
what the hell are aerosols?

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.190
And the scientists are like,

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.530
aerosols are just like the rocket ship,

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.900
but they're actually more
like that little capsule

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.660
that always breaks off of the rocket ship

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:34.610
when all of the fuel
parts of the rocket ship

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.120
fall back down to earth.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.410
This little capsule keeps
flying out into space,

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:42.510
it's way smaller and it's just floating,

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.300
not subject to gravity.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:47.680
Okay so back to this guy,
he's not even sneezing now

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.244
he's just talking or
breathing and all of this

00:02:51.244 --> 00:02:54.400
is the aerosols that
are emitting out of him,

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.660
propelled in a specific direction.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:59.020
It turns out that even
after the droplets fall

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:00.860
to the ground and onto surfaces,

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.570
the virus can stay in their little hatch,

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:06.580
these little air cells
and can float around

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:08.390
never falling to the ground.

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:10.810
This is really important
in understanding masks

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.310
and I will explain why in a minute,

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:16.810
but first we have to unpack
a second big takeaway

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:18.840
I've learned while looking into masks,

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.610
which is that number two:

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:24.000
the less virus you get,
the less sick you get.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.660
This is what I think actually

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:28.060
most people get wrong about the virus

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:30.520
and it's actually something
that I did not understand

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:32.450
until I delved into the science here.

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:34.290
- Still trying to noodle over

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:38.300
what the implications of
these findings are for masks

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.390
and which type of masks one needs.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:42.690
- I'd always thought that
if you catch the virus,

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:44.910
you're gonna get sick and how sick you get

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:47.610
depends on how good your immune system is.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.190
It turns out that's not the case.

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:52.010
COVID-19 the disease and all viruses

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.890
and their effects are not binary.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:56.490
It's not that you just get
them or you don't get them.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.710
You can get really bad
symptoms, sometimes deadly,

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:01.940
and then you can have really mild symptoms

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:05.250
and you can even have it
with zero symptoms at all.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.210
And one major thing that determines

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:10.550
how bad those symptoms
are, is your viral load.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.310
How much of the virus gets into your body?

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:15.780
If a really small amount gets in,

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:19.100
it's likely that your symptoms
will be very, very low.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:20.850
The more virus you get exposed to

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:22.460
the worst your sickness is,

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:24.280
this isn't actually a new concept.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.170
This is something they've
known since the thirties.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:29.610
I guess I didn't know it
and so I always figured

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.510
it's an on or off switch.

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:32.920
You either get it or you don't

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:34.170
and that's just not the case.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.740
So yeah, it's a pretty
simple thing to understand.

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:39.300
I mean, you could go sit through
the hour and a half lecture

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.960
that I did from University of California,

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:44.410
or you could even dive into
the studies that they did

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:47.380
where they put literal masks on hamsters,

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:49.160
or you could just take it from me.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:51.530
The less virus you get,
the less sick you get.

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:53.490
Okay so we have these
two pieces of the puzzle.

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:57.410
Number one, the virus can
break away from the droplets

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:59.520
and turn into aerosols
and float through the air

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:00.830
and get into your body.

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:05.270
And number two, the more virus
you get, the sicker you get.

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:07.520
Okay, let's put those two together

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:09.933
to figure out what masks actually do.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.923
So number three, what masks actually do.

00:05:14.923 --> 00:05:18.290
Once you put on your
mask, something happens

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.560
to everything coming out of your mouth.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.220
Let's go back to our space
analogy for a second.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.610
Putting on your mask is like increasing

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:29.940
the gravitational force
of your proverbial planet.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:32.720
Imagine how hard it would
be for a rocket ship

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:35.310
to escape Earth's gravity
and get into space

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:39.420
if the gravity were five
or 10 times more intense.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:43.260
So what a mask actually
does is it just increases

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:46.592
the gravity of your proverbial planet,

00:05:46.592 --> 00:05:50.370
makes it a lot harder
for something to launch

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:52.070
from you into the world.

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:54.820
Lots of those droplets
get caught in the mask

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.530
so that stops them from even
escaping in the first place.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:59.610
But the ones that do
escape and the aerosols

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.910
that escape from them and
have way less momentum,

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:04.370
they slow down the trajectory.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:06.540
They create different currents and eddies

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:08.690
that keep them in your sphere.

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:10.630
So if you're infected and the virus

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:12.570
gets out of your body in
one of these aerosols,

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:16.130
it's much less likely to spread
to the people around you.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:18.963
It's just like you have
a bunch more gravity.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:24.330
You can see this with this
aerosol photography technique.

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:27.590
The person with the mask is
talking just like the person

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:30.820
without, their aerosols
are still escaping,

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:32.090
but they get slowed down

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:34.500
and they stay within the
orbit of the speaker,

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:37.290
as opposed to getting
sprayed out into the world.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:40.710
Masks help direct the
particles in aerosols

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:42.520
that make it out of your breath

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:45.460
when you are talking or
coughing or sneezing.

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:47.700
So does this mean that you
should only wear a mask

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:51.150
if you are sick and you
have virus to project out

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:52.390
into the world?

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:53.930
Which is sort of what
they told us at first.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:54.763
Let's be honest.

00:06:54.763 --> 00:06:57.890
No, it turns out there is
science now that shows us

00:06:57.890 --> 00:07:00.530
what masks do to people
who are not infected.

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:02.820
Let's talk about that
second piece of the puzzle

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:03.653
that I discovered,

00:07:03.653 --> 00:07:06.960
which is the more virus you
get, the sicker you get.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:10.160
Inevitably, some virus
from a droplet or aerosol

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:11.970
will make it out of someone's mask.

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:15.240
Even if they have a really good
mask on some will come out,

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.700
let's say you're nearby when that happens.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:19.520
If you are wearing a mask,

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:22.100
there's no way that your
mask is going to filter out

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:24.320
all of those aerosols and
all of those particles,

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:25.600
those particles are too small,

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:26.700
they'll slip through your mask,

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:29.140
but if you are wearing
a mask, inevitably some

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:31.940
of those particles will get blocked

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:34.590
leading to less virus
getting into your body,

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.610
which can actually mean
way less severe symptoms,

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:40.970
which could in some
cases mean saving a life.

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:44.100
Even if it's just 50% that gets blocked,

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:47.350
that could have a major
effect on how sick you get.

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:50.720
In addition masks protect me from myself.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:52.140
I tend to touch my face

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:54.990
like a lot of humans
do just subconsciously.

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:58.150
When you're wearing a mask,
you don't actually have access

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:01.600
to the vital parts of your
face that could let virus in.

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:03.870
So if you're touching
doorknobs or other surfaces

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:05.110
that are communal,

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:08.090
you suddenly have a
blockade from yourself,

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:10.910
which leads me to my last
and most important point,

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:14.340
which is that the math of masks is insane.

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:15.950
- You really better wear a mask.

00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:20.560
- So quick recap: masks
make the proverbial gravity

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:23.630
around your proverbial
planet more intense,

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:25.130
so that it's much harder for things

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:29.090
to escape your orbit and for
the wearer who is non-infected,

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:32.290
it helps stop some particles
from getting into your system

00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:35.920
leading to a smaller viral
dose, leading to less symptoms.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:39.020
So imagine if all of these
forces come together,

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:42.320
you're in a public space and
everyone is wearing a mask.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:44.790
Everyone is getting the
gravitational effect.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.950
They're getting the filtering effect.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:49.840
They're getting the don't
touch my face effect all

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:51.430
for themselves and for each other,

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:54.820
when this happens
something magical occurs.

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:57.400
Aatish Bhatia and Minute
Physics did a collaboration

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:00.930
on this to unpack the
probability math that occurs

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:03.010
when everyone wears a mask.

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:05.310
- [Teacher] Masks are also
complicated because they fly

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:07.400
in the face of our mathematical intuition.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:09.850
- What they show is that
there's this multiplier effect

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:11.780
that when everyone is wearing a mask,

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:15.670
the probabilities of transmission
go down dramatically,

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:17.350
more than you would ever expect.

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:19.550
This helps me understand a major question

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:21.970
I had while I've been
in the COVID-19 data.

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:24.700
Mainly when I was working
on the New York Times piece,

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:27.410
I was looking at all this
data, especially in Asia,

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:29.760
a lot of these countries who
are used to wearing masks

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:34.760
like Taiwan or China or Vietnam
had really low death rates,

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:37.260
even if they had high cases

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:40.120
and that's because a lot
of people wore masks.

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:42.100
And so people who did transmit the virus

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:45.550
in these crowded places
got less dose of it

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:49.340
and became less sick and fewer
people died because of it.

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:53.010
Wearing masks truly does save lives.

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:54.120
And here in the United States,

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:56.090
if we had taken this seriously

00:09:56.090 --> 00:10:01.090
and really had solid leadership
on wearing masks 100%,

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:04.150
we probably could have
saved a lot of lives.

00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:07.360
So masks are here to stay
for the foreseeable future,

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:08.640
which is sort of waking me up

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:12.480
to the fact that I need a
comfortable, effective mask.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:13.313
I don't really have one.

